27. INTERVIEW SUMMARY
============================================================

If asked:

"What have you implemented so far?"

Answer:

"I built a real-time video analytics pipeline using YOLO for
object detection and ByteTrack for persistent multi-object
tracking. For each tracked vehicle, I extract centroid
positions and maintain a sliding trajectory history. From
consecutive positions I derive horizontal and vertical
displacement, movement magnitude, and acceleration-like
features. These features are organized into 30-frame temporal
sequences, which form the input foundation for the LSTM-based
temporal anomaly detection stage."


If asked:

"Why use trajectory information?"

Answer:

"Because an accident is a temporal event. A single frame can
identify vehicles, but the sequence of their movements can
reveal abnormal behavior such as sudden changes in direction,
motion or interactions."


If asked:

"Why use an LSTM?"

Answer:

"LSTM is designed for sequential data and can learn temporal
dependencies across multiple observations. In our system, it
will analyze sequences of vehicle motion features rather than
making an anomaly decision from a single frame."


If asked:

"Is your speed real-world speed?"

Answer:

"No. At the current stage it is image-space displacement per
frame. Converting it to physical speed would require camera
calibration or scene-scale information."


If asked:

"Why not simply use acceleration thresholds?"

Answer:

"Because a large motion change does not necessarily indicate
an accident. It can result from perspective, tracking noise,
or normal driving. A temporal model can consider the behavior
across multiple frames and reduce reliance on a single
threshold."


